---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Navigation from '../../../components/Navigation.astro';

const baseUrl = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const base = baseUrl.endsWith('/') ? baseUrl : `${baseUrl}/`;
---

<Layout>
  <Navigation />
  <div class="container">
    <article class="chapter">
      <header class="chapter-header">
        <h1>Flutter Development for macOS</h1>
        <p class="subtitle">A comprehensive guide to building native macOS applications with Flutter</p>
      </header>

      <div class="chapter-content">
        <p>Flutter is Google's UI toolkit for building natively compiled applications for mobile, web, and desktop from a single codebase. With Flutter's macOS support, you can create beautiful, performant desktop applications for Apple's Mac computers.</p>

        <h2>Why Flutter for macOS?</h2>
        <p>Flutter offers several advantages for macOS development:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Single Codebase:</strong> Write once and deploy to macOS, iOS, Android, Web, Windows, and Linux</li>
          <li><strong>Native Performance:</strong> Compiles to native ARM64 and x64 code, delivering excellent performance</li>
          <li><strong>Beautiful UI:</strong> Material Design and Cupertino widgets out of the box, plus custom theming</li>
          <li><strong>Hot Reload:</strong> See changes instantly during development</li>
          <li><strong>Rich Ecosystem:</strong> Access to thousands of packages via pub.dev</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Prerequisites</h2>
        <p>Before you begin developing Flutter apps for macOS, ensure you have:</p>
        
        <h3>1. macOS System Requirements</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>macOS 10.14 (Mojave) or later</li>
          <li>Xcode 12 or later (for iOS development and tools)</li>
          <li>Xcode Command Line Tools</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>2. Install Flutter SDK</h3>
        <p>Download and install Flutter using one of these methods:</p>
        
        <h4>Option A: Using Homebrew (Recommended)</h4>
        <pre><code>brew install --cask flutter</code></pre>

        <h4>Option B: Manual Installation</h4>
        <ol>
          <li>Download Flutter SDK from <a href="https://flutter.dev/docs/get-started/install/macos" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">flutter.dev</a></li>
          <li>Extract the archive to your desired location (e.g., <code>~/development/flutter</code>)</li>
          <li>Add Flutter to your PATH by adding this line to your <code>~/.zshrc</code> or <code>~/.bash_profile</code>:
            <pre><code>export PATH="$PATH:$HOME/development/flutter/bin"</code></pre>
          </li>
          <li>Reload your shell configuration:
            <pre><code>source ~/.zshrc  # or source ~/.bash_profile</code></pre>
          </li>
        </ol>

        <h3>3. Verify Installation</h3>
        <p>Run Flutter doctor to check your setup:</p>
        <pre><code>flutter doctor</code></pre>
        <p>This command checks your environment and displays a report of the status of your Flutter installation. You should see checks for:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Flutter SDK</li>
          <li>Android toolchain</li>
          <li>Xcode</li>
          <li>Chrome (for web development)</li>
          <li>VS Code or Android Studio (optional but recommended)</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Enable macOS Desktop Support</h2>
        <p>Flutter supports multiple platforms, but macOS desktop support may need to be enabled. To enable it:</p>
        <pre><code>flutter config --enable-macos-desktop</code></pre>

        <h2>Creating Your First macOS Flutter App</h2>

        <h3>1. Create a New Flutter Project</h3>
        <pre><code>flutter create my_macos_app</code></pre>
        <p>This creates a new Flutter project with support for all platforms. Navigate into the project:</p>
        <pre><code>cd my_macos_app</code></pre>

        <h3>2. Run on macOS</h3>
        <p>To run your app on macOS, use:</p>
        <pre><code>flutter run -d macos</code></pre>
        <p>Or if you have multiple devices, Flutter will prompt you to select one:</p>
        <pre><code>flutter run</code></pre>
        <p>Flutter will automatically detect available macOS devices and let you choose.</p>

        <h2>Project Structure</h2>
        <p>When you create a Flutter project with macOS support, you'll see this structure:</p>
        <pre><code>my_macos_app/
├── lib/
│   └── main.dart          # Your main application code
├── macos/
│   ├── Runner/
│   │   ├── Assets.xcassets/
│   │   ├── DebugProfile.entitlements
│   │   ├── Info.plist
│   │   └── MainFlutterWindow.swift
│   ├── Runner.xcodeproj/
│   └── Runner.xcworkspace/
├── pubspec.yaml           # Dependencies and assets
└── README.md</code></pre>

        <h2>macOS-Specific Configuration</h2>

        <h3>Info.plist</h3>
        <p>The <code>macos/Runner/Info.plist</code> file contains macOS-specific settings:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>CFBundleName:</strong> Application name</li>
          <li><strong>CFBundleIdentifier:</strong> Unique bundle ID (e.g., <code>com.example.myMacOSApp</code>)</li>
          <li><strong>CFBundleVersion:</strong> Version number</li>
          <li><strong>LSMinimumSystemVersion:</strong> Minimum macOS version required</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Entitlements</h3>
        <p>macOS apps may need specific entitlements for features like:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Network access</li>
          <li>File system access</li>
          <li>Camera/Microphone</li>
          <li>User notifications</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Configure these in <code>macos/Runner/DebugProfile.entitlements</code> and <code>macos/Runner/Release.entitlements</code>.</p>

        <h2>Building for macOS</h2>

        <h3>Debug Build</h3>
        <pre><code>flutter build macos --debug</code></pre>
        <p>Creates a debug build in <code>build/macos/Build/Products/Debug/</code></p>

        <h3>Release Build</h3>
        <pre><code>flutter build macos --release</code></pre>
        <p>Creates an optimized release build. The app bundle will be in <code>build/macos/Build/Products/Release/</code></p>

        <h3>Creating a DMG (Disk Image)</h3>
        <p>To distribute your macOS app, you'll want to create a DMG file. While Flutter doesn't create DMGs automatically, you can use tools like:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>create-dmg:</strong> A command-line tool for creating DMG files</li>
          <li><strong>DMG Canvas:</strong> A GUI application for creating professional DMGs</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Install create-dmg with:</p>
        <pre><code>brew install create-dmg</code></pre>
        <p>Then create a DMG from your app bundle:</p>
        <pre><code>create-dmg \
  --volname "My App" \
  --window-pos 200 120 \
  --window-size 800 400 \
  --icon-size 100 \
  --icon "My App.app" 200 190 \
  --hide-extension "My App.app" \
  --app-drop-link 600 185 \
  "MyApp.dmg" \
  "build/macos/Build/Products/Release/My App.app"</code></pre>

        <h2>Key Flutter Packages for macOS</h2>
        <p>Several packages enhance macOS development:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>window_manager:</strong> Control window size, position, and behavior</li>
          <li><strong>file_picker:</strong> Native file picking dialogs</li>
          <li><strong>menubar:</strong> Create menu bar applications</li>
          <li><strong>desktop_window:</strong> Window management utilities</li>
          <li><strong>path_provider:</strong> Get common directory paths (Documents, Downloads, etc.)</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Design Considerations for macOS</h2>

        <h3>1. Window Management</h3>
        <p>macOS apps typically support:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Multiple windows</li>
          <li>Window resizing and positioning</li>
          <li>Full-screen mode</li>
          <li>Window state restoration</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>2. Menu Bar</h3>
        <p>macOS applications have a menu bar at the top of the screen. Flutter provides access to the menu bar through platform channels or packages like <code>menubar</code>.</p>

        <h3>3. Keyboard Shortcuts</h3>
        <p>macOS users expect standard keyboard shortcuts:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>⌘Q: Quit application</li>
          <li>⌘W: Close window</li>
          <li>⌘, (Command + Comma): Preferences</li>
          <li>⌘N: New window</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Handle these with Flutter's <code>Shortcuts</code> and <code>Actions</code> widgets.</p>

        <h3>4. Native Feel</h3>
        <p>Consider using Cupertino widgets for a more native macOS feel:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><code>CupertinoButton</code></li>
          <li><code>CupertinoTextField</code></li>
          <li><code>CupertinoNavigationBar</code></li>
          <li><code>CupertinoAlertDialog</code></li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Testing on macOS</h2>
        <p>Flutter provides excellent testing support:</p>
        
        <h3>Unit Tests</h3>
        <pre><code>flutter test</code></pre>

        <h3>Integration Tests</h3>
        <pre><code>flutter drive --target=test_driver/app.dart</code></pre>

        <h3>Widget Tests</h3>
        <p>Test individual widgets in isolation:</p>
        <pre><code>flutter test test/widget_test.dart</code></pre>

        <h2>Debugging Tips</h2>
        <p>If you encounter build issues or dependency problems, try this cleanup sequence:</p>
        <pre><code>flutter clean
rm -rf .dart_tool
flutter pub get</code></pre>
        <p>This sequence will:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Clean all build artifacts</li>
          <li>Remove the Dart tool cache directory</li>
          <li>Re-download and resolve all dependencies</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Other useful debugging techniques:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Hot Reload:</strong> Press <code>r</code> in the terminal running <code>flutter run</code></li>
          <li><strong>Hot Restart:</strong> Press <code>R</code> to restart the app</li>
          <li><strong>DevTools:</strong> Open Flutter DevTools for performance profiling</li>
          <li><strong>Xcode Console:</strong> Check Xcode console for native macOS logs</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Code Signing and Distribution</h2>
        
        <h3>Apple Developer Account</h3>
        <p>For distribution outside the Mac App Store, you need:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Apple Developer account ($99/year)</li>
          <li>App-specific password</li>
          <li>Code signing certificate</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Code Signing</h3>
        <p>Sign your app before distribution:</p>
        <pre><code>codesign --force --deep --sign "Developer ID Application: Your Name" \
  "build/macos/Build/Products/Release/My App.app"</code></pre>

        <h3>Notarization</h3>
        <p>For macOS Catalina and later, apps must be notarized by Apple. Submit your app for notarization:</p>
        <pre><code>xcrun notarytool submit "MyApp.dmg" \
  --apple-id "your@email.com" \
  --password "app-specific-password" \
  --team-id "YOUR_TEAM_ID" \
  --wait</code></pre>

        <h2>Best Practices</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Responsive Design:</strong> Support various window sizes and screen resolutions</li>
          <li><strong>Accessibility:</strong> Use Flutter's accessibility widgets and test with VoiceOver</li>
          <li><strong>Performance:</strong> Profile your app with Flutter DevTools</li>
          <li><strong>Memory Management:</strong> Be mindful of memory usage, especially with large datasets</li>
          <li><strong>File Permissions:</strong> Request appropriate permissions for file system access</li>
          <li><strong>Error Handling:</strong> Provide meaningful error messages to users</li>
          <li><strong>Platform-Specific Code:</strong> Use platform channels when you need native macOS APIs</li>
        </ol>

        <h2>Resources</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Flutter Documentation:</strong> <a href="https://docs.flutter.dev/development/platform-integration/desktop" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Desktop Development</a></li>
          <li><strong>Flutter macOS Sample:</strong> <a href="https://github.com/flutter/samples/tree/main/desktop_photo_search" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Desktop Photo Search</a></li>
          <li><strong>pub.dev:</strong> <a href="https://pub.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Flutter Package Repository</a></li>
          <li><strong>Flutter Community:</strong> <a href="https://flutter.dev/community" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Community Resources</a></li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Next Steps</h2>
        <p>Now that you have the basics down, consider:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Exploring platform channels for deeper macOS integration</li>
          <li>Implementing advanced window management</li>
          <li>Adding native macOS menu bar items</li>
          <li>Integrating with macOS services (Spotlight, Quick Look, etc.)</li>
          <li>Publishing to the Mac App Store</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .container {
    max-width: 900px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 3rem 2rem;
  }

  .chapter-header {
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .chapter-header h1 {
    font-size: 2.5rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: #111827;
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
  }

  .subtitle {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    color: #6b7280;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .chapter-content {
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: #374151;
  }

  .chapter-content h2 {
    font-size: 1.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: #111827;
    margin: 2.5rem 0 1rem;
    border-bottom: 2px solid #e5e7eb;
    padding-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .chapter-content h3 {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: #111827;
    margin: 2rem 0 1rem;
  }

  .chapter-content h4 {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: #374151;
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0.75rem;
  }

  .chapter-content p {
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    font-size: 1.125rem;
  }

  .chapter-content ul,
  .chapter-content ol {
    margin: 1rem 0 1.5rem;
    padding-left: 1.5rem;
  }

  .chapter-content li {
    margin: 0.75rem 0;
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .chapter-content ul ul,
  .chapter-content ol ol {
    margin: 0.5rem 0;
  }

  .chapter-content code {
    background: #f3f4f6;
    padding: 0.125rem 0.375rem;
    border-radius: 4px;
    font-size: 0.9em;
    font-family: 'Monaco', 'Menlo', 'Ubuntu Mono', monospace;
    color: #dc2626;
  }

  .chapter-content pre {
    background: #1f2937;
    color: #f9fafb;
    padding: 1.5rem;
    border-radius: 8px;
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .chapter-content pre code {
    background: transparent;
    padding: 0;
    color: inherit;
    font-size: inherit;
  }

  .chapter-content a {
    color: #3b82f6;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .chapter-content a:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  @media screen and (max-width: 768px) {
    .container {
      padding: 2rem 1rem;
    }

    .chapter-header h1 {
      font-size: 2rem;
    }

    .subtitle {
      font-size: 1rem;
    }

    .chapter-content h2 {
      font-size: 1.5rem;
    }

    .chapter-content h3 {
      font-size: 1.25rem;
    }

    .chapter-content p,
    .chapter-content li {
      font-size: 1rem;
    }

    .chapter-content pre {
      font-size: 0.875rem;
      padding: 1rem;
    }
  }
</style>
